{"tool_name":"dsk","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"dsk","version":"2.3.3","home":"https://github.com/GATB/dsk","license":"AGPL-3.0-or-later","summary":"DSK is a k-mer counter for reads or genomes.","identifiers":["biotools:dsk","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btt020"],"license_file":"LICENSE","build":{"number":7,"run_exports":[null]},"requirements":{"build":["make","cmake <4",null,null,"pkg-config"],"host":["zlib","cppunit"]},"source_url":"https://github.com/GATB/dsk/archive/v2.3.3.tar.gz","test":{"commands":["dsk -help"]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"dsk","identifiers":["biotools:dsk","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btt020"],"license":"AGPL-3.0","summary":"DSK is a k-mer counter for reads or genomes.","home_url":"https://github.com/GATB/dsk/","keywords":["Genome, Human","Humans","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Sequence Analysis, RNA","Sequence analysis"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":8},"biotools":{"id":"dsk","home":"http://minia.genouest.org/dsk/","license":"CECILL-2.0","summary":"K-mer counting software, similar to Jellyfish. Jellyfish is very fast but limited to large-memory servers and k 32. This software supports large values of k, and runs with (almost-)arbitrarily low memory usage and reasonably low temporary disk usage. It can count k-mers of large Illumina datasets on laptops and desktop computers.","addition_date":"2015-01-21T15:12:21Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:32:52.772646Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["1.6906"],"collections":["GATB"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:dsk","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"chikhi@psu.edu","name":"Rayan Chikhi","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"guillaume.rizk@irisa.fr","name":"Guillaume Rizk","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"lavenier@irisa.fr","name":"Dominique Lavenier","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"GenOuest","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://minia.genouest.org/dsk/"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Nucleic acid sequence (raw)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2975"},"format":[{"term":"FASTQ-like format (text)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2182"}]}],"note":"K-mer counting","operation":[{"term":"Sequence composition calculation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0236"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence composition report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1261"}}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"DSK","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"GenOuest","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btt020","metadata":{"abstract":"Summary: Counting all the k-mers (substrings of length k) in DNA/RNA sequencing reads is the preliminary step of many bioinformatics applications. However, state of the art k-mer counting methods require that a large data structure resides in memory. Such structure typically grows with the number of distinct k-mers to count. We present a new streaming algorithm for k-mer counting, called DSK (disk streaming of k-mers), which only requires a fixed user-defined amount of memory and disk space. This approach realizes a memory, time and disk trade-off. The multi-set of all k-mers present in the reads is partitioned, and partitions are saved to disk. Then, each partition is separately loaded in memory in a temporary hash table. The k-mer counts are returned by traversing each hash table. Low-abundance k-mers are optionally filtered. DSK is the first approach that is able to count all the 27-mers of a human genome dataset using only 4.0 GB of memory and moderate disk space (160 GB), in 17.9 h. DSK can replace a popular k-mer counting software (Jellyfish) on small-memory servers. \u00a9 2013 The Author Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved.","authors":[{"name":"Chikhi R."},{"name":"Lavenier D."},{"name":"Rizk G."}],"citationCount":221,"date":"2013-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"DSK: K-mer counting with very low memory usage"},"pmid":"23325618","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"DSK","home":"https://bio.tools/dsk","license":"CECILL-2.0","version":"1.6906","summary":"K-mer counting software, similar to Jellyfish. Jellyfish is very fast but limited to large-memory servers and k 32. This software supports large values of k, and runs with (almost-)arbitrarily low memory usage and reasonably low temporary disk usage. It can count k-mers of large Illumina datasets on laptops and desktop computers.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:dsk:1.6906/cmd/minia.genouest.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":false},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":116,"citations":[{"count":2,"year":"2013"},{"count":2,"year":"2026"},{"count":4,"year":"2025"},{"count":5,"year":"2015"},{"count":8,"year":"2016"},{"count":9,"year":"2017"},{"count":9,"year":"2019"},{"count":9,"year":"2020"},{"count":10,"year":"2018"},{"count":10,"year":"2023"},{"count":10,"year":"2024"},{"count":12,"year":"2014"},{"count":12,"year":"2022"},{"count":14,"year":"2021"}],"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btt020","pmid":"23325618","ref_count":0,"refs":[],"title":"DSK: k-mer counting with very low memory usage.","year":2013}]}],"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright":false,"https":true,"license":false,"operational":200,"redirects":[{"status":302,"url":"https://minia.genouest.org/dsk/"}],"ssl":true,"ssl_err":null}},"support":{"email":true}},{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/bioconda:dsk:2.2.0/cmd/github.com","@licen